1
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,720
Right, so welcome
back, everyone.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,600
So after we've
seen how to define

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:11,520
and simulate neurons, neural
masses, and external sources,

4
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:17,450
now we basically are
going to combine elements

5
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:20,150
of these types, these three
types, in different combinations

6
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,580
to create circuits.

7
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:27,200
And the outline for this
session is essentially,

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,050
we're going to go through two
examples from the literature.

9
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:34,970
The first one is from our own
lab, developed by Anand here

10
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,610
leading it, and that
is a biomimetic model

11
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:40,130
of corticostriatal
micro assemblies.

12
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:43,850
You've seen the full model in
[INAUDIBLE] on the first day

13
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:45,240
doing category learning.

14
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,922
Today, you will build
a subset of that model,

15
00:00:47,922 --> 00:00:49,880
and you will see how the
hierarchical structure

16
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,660
in neural blocks actually works.

17
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:54,930
And the second example
is a PIG model,

18
00:00:54,930 --> 00:01:00,600
And the pig stands for Pyramidal
Interneuron Gamma rhythms.

19
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:05,080
So that's a network of pyramidal
neurons and interneurons

20
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:10,360
interacting with each other
and generating gamma rhythms.

21
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:16,480
So first, let's move on to
the corticostriatal model.

22
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,110
So yeah, as I was saying, this
is-- you've seen the full model,

23
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:24,310
and the full model was based
on mechanistic principles

24
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:27,400
about how the brain and
behavior should actually

25
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:31,370
incorporate any interrelations
across multiple scales.

26
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:37,010
And the multiple scales here are
starting from neural receptors,

27
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,290
right?

28
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:40,330
Neural activity of
individual neurons.

29
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:42,790
And then up to behavior
because this model

30
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:47,030
is able to both take in
input from the outside world

31
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:50,440
in the form of images
and also output actions,

32
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:52,510
left to right decisions.

33
00:01:52,510 --> 00:01:56,895
And it works across
these three scales.

34
00:01:56,895 --> 00:01:58,270
And yeah, here,
you can find also

35
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:00,140
the reference to this model.

36
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:03,210
And this model was,
well, the first big model

37
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:05,890
to be designed entirely
within Neuroblox.

38
00:02:09,252 --> 00:02:10,960
You probably have seen
this graph before,

39
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:13,260
and this is the graph
of the complete model,

40
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:17,740
incorporating both cortical
components, two of them,

41
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:21,420
and also subcortical components,
multiple different ones,

42
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,730
each one serving a
different function.

43
00:02:23,730 --> 00:02:28,350
A subset of these you will
see in the exercises today.

44
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:31,230
So this model has been--

45
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:36,130
there's a lot of biophysical
knowledge given into this model.

46
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:39,660
So it has been constrained by
biology, both in its parameters

47
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,990
and also in its structure,
in how the blocks connect

48
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:44,012
with each other.

49
00:02:44,012 --> 00:02:45,720
You will see that in
the connection rules

50
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:47,050
for cortical blocks.

51
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:51,420
And after it has been
built like this, following

52
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,970
any constraints that we
have from the literature,

53
00:02:53,970 --> 00:02:59,250
from biology, it exhibits
this bottom-up emergence where

54
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:05,000
actually, it matches observed
behavior and brain dynamics

55
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,390
starting from cell level
physiology in the macaque brain.

56
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:11,570
So it's important to note
that this model has never

57
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:14,490
been fitted to date
to macaque data,

58
00:03:14,490 --> 00:03:16,130
but it can still
match data, both

59
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:18,110
on the
electrophysiological level

60
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:19,770
and also on the
behavioral level.

61
00:03:19,770 --> 00:03:22,610
And the story of the
model is essentially

62
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:26,180
moving from synapses, to
local assemblies of neurons,

63
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:29,430
to brain regions, and to
bigger brain circuits.

64
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:32,510
And in fact, you will
move across these scales

65
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:34,360
in the first exercise today.

66
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:40,170
So as I was saying, you will
build a subset of this model.

67
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:43,790
This subset will include
two cortical components,

68
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:47,640
one for the visual cortex and
one for the arterial cortex.

69
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:50,270
And you will also add an
ascending system component

70
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:55,460
that's brainstem modulating the
spiking rhythm for the cortex.

71
00:03:55,460 --> 00:04:00,260
And also later on, you will
add the image stimulus source

72
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:00,925
to this.

73
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:07,380
So you will build the
model from the bottom up.

74
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:09,120
So you will start
from single neurons.

75
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,330
And at the smallest
level we have

76
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:15,660
this superficial cortical
layer for lateral inhibition.

77
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,570
You can see a schematic
of this down here

78
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:22,160
where we have essentially four
excitatory neurons and one

79
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:24,450
feedback interneuron.

80
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:26,270
And as you can see,
all excited neurons

81
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:29,240
connect to the interneuron,
and the interneuron

82
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:30,930
connects back to all of them.

83
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:33,890
And this establishes
this type of winner

84
00:04:33,890 --> 00:04:36,170
take all competition
where as you

85
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:39,180
can see in this stacked plot
here-- this, by the way,

86
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:42,480
is a plot that you can generate
entirely within Neuroblox.

87
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,120
You will see how.

88
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:49,440
You can see that the interneuron
remains tonically active,

89
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:54,720
and some of the
excitatory neurons spiked.

90
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:57,240
The initial conditions
are randomized here.

91
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:01,110
But only one,
because it spiked--

92
00:05:01,110 --> 00:05:03,660
as you can see, it's spiked
more frequently to begin with.

93
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:05,210
And so this one wins.

94
00:05:05,210 --> 00:05:11,600
And these spike timings that
were more frequent to begin with

95
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,950
enabled this one
to keep spiking.

96
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:15,700
And because it connects
to the interneuron

97
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:18,740
and the interneuron connects to
all other excitatory neurons,

98
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:22,000
then the interneuron
shuts down the rest

99
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,800
of the excitatory neurons.

100
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,470
So this is the winner takes all.

101
00:05:26,470 --> 00:05:31,060
The last excitatory neuron
has clearly won in this case.

102
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:35,050
And you can do all sorts
of loads of functions

103
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:39,440
by stating multiple of
these assemblies together,

104
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,510
and you will see how that
works in learning as well.

105
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:48,192
Now, as I was saying, it's
only the pyramidal neuron

106
00:05:48,192 --> 00:05:50,650
essentially with the strongest
input at a given time that's

107
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:53,080
allowed to spike,
and everything else

108
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:56,740
is being inhibited by this
single inhibitory neuron.

109
00:05:56,740 --> 00:06:00,160
So, moving one layer
up in the hierarchy,

110
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,860
we will connect
multiple of these winner

111
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,300
takes all blocks together.

112
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:08,200
And essentially, that
constitutes a cortical block.

113
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,320
So in this schematic,
you can see

114
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:13,950
it's essentially six of these
winner take all blocks connected

115
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:14,620
together.

116
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:18,420
Again, you will see the
connection rule between them.

117
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:20,880
And yeah, again,
here is a schematic

118
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:23,070
of a stacked plot
where you can see

119
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:30,030
that it's usually one
excitatory neuron winning over

120
00:06:30,030 --> 00:06:31,690
the rest across.

121
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:33,790
its WTA block, right?

122
00:06:33,790 --> 00:06:36,272
So the blue colors are
always excitatory neurons,

123
00:06:36,272 --> 00:06:38,105
and the red ones are
the inhibitory neurons.

124
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:45,100
So, keep going up
on the hierarchy,

125
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:49,060
we will connect two
cortical blocks together.

126
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:52,810
It's one holding these multiple
winner takes all circuits.

127
00:06:52,810 --> 00:06:55,037
One cortical block will
represent visual cortex,

128
00:06:55,037 --> 00:06:57,120
and the other one is going
to present the anterior

129
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,072
cortex connected one way--
so, visual cortex to anterior.

130
00:07:02,072 --> 00:07:04,530
Of course, there's going to be
an image stimulus connecting

131
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:05,920
to the visual cortex.

132
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,170
And finally, there
is the brainstem.

133
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:10,250
This is the ascending
system input

134
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:13,460
that actually connects
onto both cortices

135
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:16,880
and modulates their activity.

136
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:19,340
And here, I'm just
showing you some examples

137
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:23,510
of essentially plotting
recipes, visualization options

138
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:25,460
that we include in
Neuroblox and that you

139
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:28,410
will use in the circuits
[INAUDIBLE] now.

140
00:07:28,410 --> 00:07:31,970
And these are particularly
useful for assemblies

141
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:34,620
or any collection of
units in a circuit.

142
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:36,810
So the stack plot, you've
seen this already, right?

143
00:07:36,810 --> 00:07:40,970
We're just plotting the
voltages of all the inputs

144
00:07:40,970 --> 00:07:41,910
that you give to it.

145
00:07:41,910 --> 00:07:44,840
So you can provide a
single composite block

146
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:45,840
to add to a stack block.

147
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,770
And it's going to look
for all the neurons

148
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:49,770
in that composite block
and plot their activity.

149
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:53,190
Or you can provide just a
vector of neurons in it,

150
00:07:53,190 --> 00:07:55,770
and it's going to plot the
activity of it's vector.

151
00:07:55,770 --> 00:07:57,830
It's one of those neurons.

152
00:07:57,830 --> 00:07:59,990
Or you can do a
mean field pull plot

153
00:07:59,990 --> 00:08:01,520
where this is just
the population

154
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:05,450
average of all the membrane
potentials and finally a power

155
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:09,910
spectrum, which is,
yeah, the average power

156
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:15,670
spectrum across all of the
units in the composite block.

157
00:08:15,670 --> 00:08:18,490
And the second example
that we will look at

158
00:08:18,490 --> 00:08:23,020
is this network of
pyramidal and interneurons

159
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:24,470
that generate gamma rhythms.

160
00:08:24,470 --> 00:08:28,383
And this model was designed
to capture attention

161
00:08:28,383 --> 00:08:29,800
with the assumption
that attention

162
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:31,370
is fundamentally rhythmic.

163
00:08:31,370 --> 00:08:35,590
So the idea is that we'll have
some excitatory neurons that

164
00:08:35,590 --> 00:08:38,270
are particularly
sensitized to some stimuli.

165
00:08:38,270 --> 00:08:42,970
And during this interaction
between the pyramidal neurons

166
00:08:42,970 --> 00:08:47,020
and the interneurons, some
of the pyramidal neurons

167
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:49,300
will align the rhythm
and actually start

168
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:52,330
this gamma rhythm
that will align

169
00:08:52,330 --> 00:08:55,220
with some external
stimulus coming in.

170
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:57,520
And that is how we
essentially pay attention

171
00:08:57,520 --> 00:08:59,540
to that stimulus
over other stimuli.

172
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:03,010
And in the original
work, this gamma rhythm

173
00:09:03,010 --> 00:09:06,860
is actually generated-- it is
entrenched inside a beta rhythm.

174
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:12,090
So the beta rhythm
essentially drives the gamma.

175
00:09:12,090 --> 00:09:16,330
And in the tutorial, you will
first generate the gamma rhythm.

176
00:09:16,330 --> 00:09:19,630
But if you want to add the beta
rhythm as well on top of it,

177
00:09:19,630 --> 00:09:22,227
you will see how to
do that, basically.

178
00:09:22,227 --> 00:09:24,060
You just need to add
an external source that

179
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:28,360
is a sinusoidal current
in the beta frequency.

180
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:36,000
Right, so essentially,
as I was saying,

181
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,090
it's the negative feedback
from the interneurons

182
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:42,810
to the pyramidal
neurons, that is,

183
00:09:42,810 --> 00:09:47,100
and the spike timings that
are essential for generating

184
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:47,920
attention.

185
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,560
And when I say attention, I
mean generating the rhythms.

186
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:53,620
And if the peak of the rhythm
aligns with the stimulus,

187
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:56,800
that's how you would
attend to that stimulus.

188
00:09:56,800 --> 00:10:00,000
That's the assumption.

189
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,910
And here, you can see another
example of this happening

190
00:10:05,910 --> 00:10:09,510
where we have the excitatory
neurons starting, well,

191
00:10:09,510 --> 00:10:11,470
perfectly synchronized.

192
00:10:11,470 --> 00:10:14,970
And through the tonic
activity of inhibitory neurons

193
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:17,230
that reciprocally
connect to them,

194
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:20,610
you will see that it's only a
subset of the excitatory neurons

195
00:10:20,610 --> 00:10:22,110
winning, right?

196
00:10:22,110 --> 00:10:25,800
And this subset
on the bottom here

197
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:29,760
is what will remain
synchronized, a few neurons

198
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:30,640
on the top as well.

199
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:35,100
But this cluster will
essentially win and stay

200
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:38,790
synchronized, and whatever
this cluster is sensitive to,

201
00:10:38,790 --> 00:10:43,020
the organism will
pay attention to it.

202
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:45,150
And of course, this
cluster can vary according

203
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:46,990
to timings for the spikes.

204
00:10:46,990 --> 00:10:52,590
This was a perfectly
aligned spike to begin with.

205
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:58,890
So for the second tutorial, we
will build this pink model based

206
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:00,280
on a paper from the literature.

207
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:02,920
And this is a
schematic for this.

208
00:11:02,920 --> 00:11:06,420
You can see two subsets
of excitatory neurons

209
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:11,250
and one population of inhibitory
neurons with these connections.

210
00:11:11,250 --> 00:11:13,140
The input here is
the external input

211
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:14,770
that to begin with is constant.

212
00:11:14,770 --> 00:11:16,680
But as I was saying,
you can change this

213
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:20,490
to some sinusoid current
to the beta rhythm as well.

214
00:11:20,490 --> 00:11:23,730
And yeah, the
[INAUDIBLE] will focus

215
00:11:23,730 --> 00:11:26,370
on how this rhythmic
inhibition is

216
00:11:26,370 --> 00:11:31,890
more effective than any type
of asynchronous inhibition.

217
00:11:31,890 --> 00:11:33,700
And this was my last slide.

218
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:36,930
This has an extra disclaimer.

219
00:11:36,930 --> 00:11:39,690
Please, for the
session first navigate

220
00:11:39,690 --> 00:11:41,118
to the Introduction page.

221
00:11:41,118 --> 00:11:42,160
This is not the notebook.

222
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:44,790
This is only on the website
because it's mainly text.

223
00:11:44,790 --> 00:11:47,590
So go to Circuit Models
and then Introduction.

224
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:50,970
And because there are some
important points here,

225
00:11:50,970 --> 00:11:54,300
particularly around
namespace in Neuroblox,

226
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:57,760
that is important to know before
you start working with circuits.

227
00:11:57,760 --> 00:12:03,930
And the long story short is
that namespace is something

228
00:12:03,930 --> 00:12:06,940
that we were asking you to
add to your custom blocks,

229
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:09,360
but we're keeping it
equal to nothing for now

230
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,330
because if we just
use neurons, there's

231
00:12:12,330 --> 00:12:15,710
no hierarchical structure
beneath that neuron.

232
00:12:15,710 --> 00:12:17,260
So you don't need
to carry around

233
00:12:17,260 --> 00:12:20,780
this namespace that qualifies
the name of every variable

234
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:22,040
and of every parameter.

235
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:24,430
But when you have
hierarchical blocks,

236
00:12:24,430 --> 00:12:27,920
like this cortical blocks will
be in both of the examples,

237
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:32,080
then you need to supply
the namespace so, in fact,

238
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:37,030
the connections are actually
generated appropriately.

239
00:12:37,030 --> 00:12:40,810
So we keep track of where
its variable is coming,

240
00:12:40,810 --> 00:12:43,000
from which neuron,
and how many terms

241
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,040
should be added to that
connect input variable

242
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:48,220
and to then be able to
substitute that input

243
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:50,390
variable into our
differential equations.

244
00:12:50,390 --> 00:12:52,190
So it's essential to
have this namespace.

245
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:56,090
So please read this namespace
section before you start.

246
00:12:56,090 --> 00:12:59,650
And then you can move on to
the corticostriatal model.

247
00:12:59,650 --> 00:13:02,830
OK, granted that these examples,
including the challenge

248
00:13:02,830 --> 00:13:04,820
problems, they can
get a bit lengthy.

